    "frames": [
      {
        "slide": 1,
        "role": "cover",
        "subject": "A tumor nest of domed oxblood cells, each with a nucleus dimple, ringed by a pale cool mucin coat that is now a wet pebbled glaze of small glossy globules catching the key on the upper-left shoulder. A dense field of ember T cells sits on the ridged plum stroma to the left and stops at the coat's outer foot.",
        "visual_consequence": "Without the headline the frame reads as a crowd of ember cells stopped at a glossy pale ring with nothing ember inside it. The coat reads as a wet coat with relief and point highlights, and it leaves a cavity shadow on the first row of cells inside its upper-left arc.",
        "weakness": "The new c24 moved the source line onto the coat's lower-left arc, where cream mono type over the glaze has less contrast than the rest of the row, still readable. The black voids in the right-hand stroma have hard ragged edges at native size.",
        "repair": "Deepen the footer wash under the left footer group until the coat under 'c24' falls to the stroma's value, and confirm the row's contrast.",
        "encoding_reads": true,
        "dominant_subject_finished": true,
        "feed_legible": true,
